## The item named one shape; a probe found three, and they are one bug

`scala.py` read a declared type with `find_child_by_type(node,
"type_identifier")`, which names ONLY a bare type. Tree-sitter gives a qualified
type its own node kind (`stable_type_identifier`) and a generic applied type
another (`generic_type`). Pairs differing in exactly one thing:

| shape | before |
|---|---|
| `val x: File` | hint `File` |
| `val x: java.io.File` | **none** |
| `val x = new File("p")` | hint `File` |
| `val x = new java.io.File("p")` | **none** |
| `val x: Buffer` | hint `Buffer` |
| `val x: Buffer[String]` | **none** |

So it is **not** the annotation path: `new` loses it identically, though the item
lists `val x = new Foo()` among the shapes that WORK — a fix scoped to the
annotation branch would have left it broken *and looked like a fix*. And a
GENERIC APPLIED type is not in the filing at all, while `List[X]` / `Option[X]` /
`Future[X]` are pervasive in idiomatic Scala.

Four sites each called `find_child_by_type(..., "type_identifier")` themselves;
they now share ONE walk, because sharing a predicate is not enough when callers
can still walk different populations (INV-motos).

