## The measurement changed the change

The first version scored **higher** on the item's own acceptance metric — 28.07%
hinted on sbt. Sampling the edges it newly RESOLVED showed why that was bad: four
of twelve were `.get` on a standard-library `Map[K,V]` binding to the PROJECT's
`Map.get` in `sbt/SessionVar.scala`. Counted rather than left as an impression:
**44 of 185 newly-resolved edges on sbt (24%)** bound to an implicitly-imported
name — the "arbitrary same-named internal def" funnel this file's external branch
already refuses for untyped receivers.

`Map` / `List` / `Set` / `Option` come from `scala.Predef._` and appear in NO
import line, so a generic base of that name misses `import_aliases`, adds nothing
to the module slot, and reaches only the short-name bind. Denying it:

| | hinted | slot | mis-binds |
|---|---|---|---|
| sbt, first version | 28.07% | 18.07% | **44 (24%)** |
| sbt, shipped | 23.43% | **18.19%** | **0** |

**The slot share rose.** An unqualifiable name never filled a slot, so the
refusal costs nothing where the catalogue is actually reached — and **the item's
own acceptance metric would have preferred the worse version.** A hint is
necessary, not sufficient (L5); here it was actively misleading as a target.

Scoped to a base this change INVENTS from a type argument. An explicitly
annotated bare `val x: Map = ...` is untouched — the programmer wrote that name,
it is the pre-existing INV-fahub population, and narrowing it is a separate
change with its own measurement.

## A pre-registered prediction, refuted

I predicted generics would contribute more than qualified names on BOTH repos.

    repo   qualified   generic
    sbt          213     2,180    generic dominates, 10.2x
    lila       1,068       284    QUALIFIED dominates, 3.8x

The two repos have opposite idioms, and a single-repo run would have "confirmed"
whichever one I happened to run.

## Two of my own instruments were wrong, and arithmetic caught both

The first attribution pass filtered on `call_construct` BEFORE checking
`is_resolved`, so an edge that became resolved looked like a disappearance — a
gain and a regression are opposite stories and it could not tell them apart. And
`decompose.py` RE-IMPLEMENTED the shipped type walk instead of calling it,
drifting the moment the denylist landed and surfacing as a **negative**
contribution. It now feeds the SHIPPED function a filtered view of a node's
children and asserts its OFF arm reproduces the published baseline (1,446 /
1,712) exactly before any decomposition is reported.

